reference:
 The NoDiscard attribute
 NoDiscard





   &reftitle.intro;

    This attribute can be used to indicate that the return value of a function
    or a method should not be discarded. If the return value is not used in any
    way, a warning will be emitted.


    This is useful for functions where not checking the return value is likely
    to be a bug, for example for functions that return a new instance and do not
    mutate the instance they are called on.


    To intentionally discard the return value of such a function, the return
    value can be cast to void.





   &reftitle.classsynopsis;



     final
     NoDiscard


    &Methods;









   &reftitle.examples;

    Basic usage


<?php

#[\NoDiscard]
function create_value(): int {
    return 42;
}

create_value();

?>


    &example.outputs;


Warning: Value returned from create_value() must be used




    Usage on methods


<?php

class Number {
    public function __construct(private int $value) {}

    #[\NoDiscard]
    public function add(int $n): self {
        return new self($this->value + $n);
    }
}

$n = new Number(42);
$n->add(1);

?>


    &example.outputs;


Warning: Value returned from Number::add() must be used




    Intentionally discarding the return value


<?php

#[\NoDiscard]
function create_value(): int {
    return 42;
}

(void) create_value();

?>


    &example.outputs.void;





